==Gravitational Dynamics and Resonant Architecture==
[[File:Aurelius_System_Orbits.png|thumb|center|800px|Radial logarithmic orbital architecture and habitable zone boundaries of the Aurelius system]]
[[File:Aurelius_Planetary_Scales.png|thumb|right|380px|Comparative planetary physical scales and masses of the twelve major worlds]]

===The Inner Clockwork: The 5:4 Quetzal–Adonia Resonance===
The dynamical keystone of the inner system is the hot gas giant [[Quetzal]] (0.862 AU), which is locked in an exact 5:4 orbital mean-motion resonance with the life-bearing world [[Adonia]] (1.00 AU):<ref name="Aldana_Equilibrium" />
$$\frac{P_\text{Adonia}}{P_\text{Quetzal}} = \frac{371.25\text{ days}}{297.00\text{ days}} = \frac{5}{4} = 1.25$$

This integer ratio dictates that for every five revolutions completed by Quetzal, Adonia completes exactly four revolutions. The two bodies encounter one another at inferior conjunction once every 1,485 standard days—exactly four Adonian calendar years. Because the conjunction geometry is phase-locked, the minimum encounter distance is constrained to approximately 0.14 AU (20.9 million km).<ref name="Aldana_Equilibrium" />

This resonance functions as a cosmic metronome for the inner solar system. Rather than destabilizing Adonia, the phase-locked encounters prevent secular eccentricity excitation, maintaining Adonia's orbit at a low, stable eccentricity ($e = 0.017$) that preserves a steady global climate over hundreds of millions of years.<ref name="Aldana_Equilibrium" /> At closest approach, Quetzal's tidal pull on Adonia reaches 3.5 times normal lunar tides, generating quadrennial King Tides that periodically flush coastal estuaries, modulate ocean currents, and gently flex the lithosphere along oceanic trenches.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" /> Furthermore, because the 1,485-day synodic cycle aligns with the four-year civil calendar, Adonian civilizations established the quadrennial intercalary leap day—'''February 31st''' (the [[Day of the Dragon]])—to coincide with Quetzal's alignment with Aurelius.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

Furthermore, Quetzal's inner gravitational resonance chain helps maintain the wide, non-crossing orbital spacing of [[Mictlán]] (0.62 AU) and [[Ciguapa]] (0.45 AU), ensuring the perpetual stability of the inner terrestrial worlds.<ref name="Aldana_Equilibrium" />

===The Outer Gravitational Sentinels===
Beyond the frost line, [[Amatista]] ($380 M_\text{Adonia}$) and [[Alcázar]] ($510 M_\text{Adonia}$) collectively contain over 890 times the mass of Adonia. Their deep gravitational wells dominate the outer system, carving wide, stable gaps in the asteroid belt and preventing the accumulation of massive planet-crossing asteroids. Together, they intercept, deflect, or swallow over 99.6% of long-period comets descending from the outer Oort reservoir, serving as planetary shields for the inner biosphere.<ref name="IACC_Overview" /> The gravitational interactions between Alcázar, Lucero, and Centinela maintain the sharp inner boundary of the trans-Centinelan Kuiper disk, while regular mean-motion resonances, such as Erebo's 3:2 resonance with Centinela, ensure that eccentric Kuiper-type outliers never collide with the outer ice giants.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

==Origin and Early Evolution==

The Aurelius system condensed approximately 4.6 billion years ago from the gravitational collapse of a giant molecular cloud fragment enriched in heavy elements by prior generations of supernovae and asymptotic giant branch (AGB) stars ($[\text{Fe}/\text{H}] = +0.04$).<ref name="IACC_Overview" />

===The Early Instability Epoch===
During the first 200 million years following the dispersal of the protoplanetary gas disk, the system underwent a period of dynamic planetary realignment. Protoplanetary collisions and intense stellar winds stripped the lighter silicate crusts of [[Tartessos]], leaving behind its oversized 85% metallic iron core.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" /> As Aurelius settled onto the main sequence and gradually brightened, Ciguapa's primordial oceans boiled away, triggering the permanent runaway greenhouse catastrophe that characterizes its modern 93-atmosphere envelope.<ref name="Aldana_Equilibrium" /> In the outer realm, proto-Alcázar suffered a catastrophic oblique collision with a wandering protoplanet of 6 to 8 Adonian masses; the impactor knocked Alcázar's rotational axis onto its side, producing its modern 97.8° obliquity, and ejected millions of cubic kilometers of volatile ice into orbit to form its sprawling ring citadel and satellite system.<ref name="Balisca_Alcazar">Baliscan Royal Astronomical Society. (2016). *Catastrophic Giant Impact Origin for Alcázar's 98-Degree Obliquity and Satellite Ring Architecture*. Journal of Comparative Planetology, 74(3), 198–229.</ref> Gravitational perturbations from Alcázar's collision and Amatista's migration simultaneously scattered billions of icy planetesimals inward toward the terrestrial zone, delivering the water, carbon, and prebiotic nitrogen compounds that formed Adonia's vast oceans and atmosphere.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

==Habitability and the Adonian Biosphere==

Among the twelve planets, [[Adonia]] is the sole world known to harbor life. Its habitability is not an isolated coincidence, but the systemic outcome of the Aurelius system's overall architectural balance. Aurelius itself is a middle-aged G0V star with a predictable 11.2-year magnetic cycle and negligible superflare activity, providing consistent irradiance ($1,710\text{ W/m}^2$) within its conservative habitable zone.<ref name="IACC_Overview" /> Locked in the 5:4 resonance with Quetzal, Adonia maintains a stable low-eccentricity orbit, while its two natural moons, [[Imera]] and [[Theia]], gravitationally anchor its 24.3° axial obliquity, preventing the chaotic climate swings that occur on single-moon worlds.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" /> Beneath the surface, a vigorous liquid iron outer core generates a powerful geomagnetic field that deflects the Aurelian wind, shielding the dense nitrogen-oxygen atmosphere and the life-giving oceans below.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

==History of Observation and Space Exploration==

The study of the Aurelius system reflects the intellectual and technological progression of Adonian civilization. In archaic times, ancient Sinopian, Sanese, and Insulonian priest-astronomers cataloged the eight classical wandering stars visible to the unaided eye: Tartessos, Ciguapa, Mictlán, Quetzal, Adonia, Aztlán, Cíbola, and Amatista, incorporating their synodic cycles into religious cosmologies and agricultural calendars.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

The telescopic revolution of the 17th to 19th centuries, spearheaded by observatories in Balisca and Okaiken, resolved planetary discs, discovered the rings of Amatista and Alcázar, and culminated in the discovery of the outer ice giants: Donato de Silva discovered [[Lucero]] in 1781, while Tanigaki Ren and Marcelo Vives mathematically predicted and located [[Centinela]] in 1846.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" /> In 1930, the photographic era enabled Sinopian astronomer Aris Thorne to discover the dwarf planet [[Erebo]] using a blink comparator at Kydonia Peak Observatory.<ref name="Tanigaki_Arch" />

In the modern space age, dedicated interplanetary robotic missions—including the *Explorer*, *Kagayaki*, *Hermes*, *Tenma*, *Deep Horizon*, and *Nyx-Erebo* programs—have executed close flybys, orbital insertions, atmospheric soundings, and robotic landings across all twelve worlds, transforming theoretical celestial mechanics into empirical planetary science.<ref name="IACC_Overview" />

